Z||000015||Patrick||01-10-2000||06:17 PM||||<BR>You might be better off to go to an auto wrecker and get a used transmission oil cooler. I suggest the oil cooler for two reasons. It would be easily accessible on the vehicle, and the most important reason, there would be no chance of it being internally rusted (as would be the case with a used heater core). As long as it looked Ok on the outside, it would probably be fine.||||reg||3
Z||000016||Wolfhound||01-11-2000||06:28 PM||||Do you plan to force cool air over the CPU or use the heater core to extract heat from a coolant jacket or plate? <BR> What about adapting one of the tiny office refrig units. Probably find one about the cost of a heater core. Whole shooting match in one bundle.||||reg||5
Z||000017||DaRkLoRD||01-11-2000||07:00 PM||||the stage 1 cooler pumps distilled water thru a water block (solid aluminum block with a channel cut thru it for the water to flow in a C-shape) which then goes through the radiator. the stage 2 cooler uses mineral oil which is cooled by thermoelectric coolers (peltiers) and then the heat is transferred from that to the radiator using antifreeze.<P>(the stage 2 device is a bit more expensive.. but it will also be a lot colder!)<P>I've checked with all the local appliance stores, they can't get a radiator that size. a radiator out of a small fridge is what I originally wanted, and they don't have any larger radiators that I could cut either..||||reg||1
